{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b15beca1a33744","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n20:The odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n26:The component packages a Workspace controller (reconciling kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks) alongside the dashboard operator. Workspaces are managed via StatefulSets with associated Services and PodDisruptionBudgets. Cert-manager provisions TLS certificates for the webhook and metrics endpoints. All Go binaries (operator and BFF sidecars) are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `-tags strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptographic operations through system OpenSSL. [source: Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:18-20, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86, packages/notebooks/upstream/workspaces/controller/internal/webhook/workspace_webhook.go:44]\n53:| Dockerfile.konflux.genai:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n79:| gen-ai BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated gen-ai backend-for-frontend service |\n97:| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n98:| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n99:| packages/gen-ai/bff/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n151:| /gen-ai/api/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n152:| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n302:| NeMo Guardrails | varies | HTTPS | TLS | API Key | Content moderation |\n453:| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | Bearer Token (x-forwarded-access-token) | Go BFF middleware (RequireAccessToService) | RBAC and namespace access |\n478:| **Build flags (Go BFFs)** | CGO_ENABLED=1, -tags strictfipsruntime | Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86 |\n479:| **Linking** | Dynamic (CGO_ENABLED=1) | Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:18, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86 |\n480:| **OpenSSL in image** | Yes (via UBI9 base images: ubi9/go-toolset, ubi9-minimal) | Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:22, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:89 |\n491:| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | None detected in Go components; protobuf duplicate-registration conflict between mlflow-go and OTel SDK suppressed at warning level via GOLANG_PROTOBUF_REGISTRATION_CONFLICT=warn | Dockerfile.konflux.genai:99-103 |\n493:All Go binaries (dashboard-operator and all BFF sidecars) are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `-tags strictfipsruntime`, ensuring dynamic linking to system OpenSSL for FIPS-validated cryptographic operations. The Node.js backend inherits FIPS-capable OpenSSL from its UBI9/nodejs-22 base image but does not have an explicit FIPS opt-in at the application level. No `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` flag is used; FIPS enforcement relies on the `-tags` build constraint. [source: Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:18-20, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86, Dockerfile.konflux:34]\n506:- **BFF sidecar traffic:** Each federated module's API calls are routed by path prefix to the corresponding Go BFF sidecar (e.g., `/gen-ai/api/*` → port 8143, `/model-registry/api/*` → port 8043, `/agent-ops/api/*` → port 8843). BFF sidecars authenticate requests independently using Bearer tokens, perform SubjectAccessReviews for authorization, and issue Kubernetes API calls using either the user's forwarded token or an internal ServiceAccount token, depending on the configured `auth-method` flag. Two Kubernetes Service objects expose the operator's metrics (port 8443) and webhook (port 443→9443) endpoints internally. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, packages/gen-ai/bff/internal/constants/api_constants.go:15, dashboard-operator/config/webhook/manifests.yaml:29]\n508:- **External service interactions:** BFF sidecars proxy requests to external platform services: gen-ai BFF connects to Llama Stack servers, MLflow tracking servers, NeMo guardrails, and MCP servers (via SSE/streamable HTTP with `X-MCP-Bearer` authentication); eval-hub BFF queries TrustyAI and KServe InferenceServices; maas BFF communicates with MaaS API servers; and the core-bff proxies to Prometheus/Thanos for metrics dashboards. All external connections use TLS encryption with Bearer token or API key authentication. [source: packages/gen-ai/bff/internal/constants/mcp.go:5-16, packages/eval-hub/bff/internal/api/inferenceservices_handler.go:94]\n560:| NeMo Guardrails | REST |  | varies | HTTPS | TLS | Content moderation |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-899742fad80db074","agent_id":"aecdf30ec811431c0","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"15f72030-e7ad-4273-bfe8-e7905818df48","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:56:26.198Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b15beca1a33744","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
