ed via the DSC for the\n    following release (each option must be able to consume a connection string rather\n    than assume self-provisioning forever);\n  - per-namespace isolation requirements for multi-tenant clusters.\n- Decide the provisioning approach and record the decision, including the fallback to\n  bring-your-own PostgreSQL (user-configured OGXServer connection details) if neither\n  self-provisioning option is viable for the release.\n- Communicate the outcome so the selected implementation path is scheduled and the\n  unselected alternatives are dropped from the plan.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written decision record exists that states the go/no-go gate criteria, the evidence\n  used (delivery plan and status of the dashboard operator modularization dependency),\n  and the selected provisioning approach.\n- The decision names exactly one selected approach (operator-pattern self-provisioning,\n  BFF-managed self-provisioning, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL) and identifies the gate\n  owner and the gate decision date.\n- The decision record documents the lifecycle-management and portability considerations\n  that informed the choice, and the fallback approach if the selected option fails\n  during implementation.\n- The decision is socialized with the Gen AI Studio team and the owning team of the\n  operator modularization dependency, and the release plan reflects the selected path.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: a default vector store is available out-of-the-box\n  without manual provisioning — this investigation selects the mechanism that delivers it.\n- Strategy dependency noted in Staff Engineer Input: RHAISTRAT-7164 is a hard dependency\n  for the operator path; the strategy cannot commit to a release until its timing is\n  resolved.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The gating input — the delivery timing of RHAISTRAT-7164 — is controlled by another\n  team and cannot be compressed by this epic. The gate decision date is a 