{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b87bed5a-1a31-41c6-a4c9-74be8457c035","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-80d087d847acb94a","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-bgxd3ey4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E003.md","content":"# Capture inline Milvus baseline and benchmark pgvector for Playground RAG workloads\n\n## Description\n\nBefore inline providers are removed from the OGX distribution (RHAISTRAT-7474), capture\nthe performance baseline of the inline Milvus vector store on representative Playground\nRAG workloads, then benchmark a pgvector-backed remote vector store on the same\nworkloads and verify it is in the same order of magnitude. The outcome is a benchmark\nreport and a concrete resource-sizing recommendation for the default PostgreSQL/pgvector\ninstance. Per the Staff Engineer Input, the default store targets development\nexperimentation: no strict latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99) apply, and typical Playground RAG\nusage involves small document sets where a flat scan (no index) is sufficient.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define representative Playground RAG benchmark workloads reflecting typical usage:\n  small document sets, ingestion (embedding + indexing) and retrieval (query) phases,\n  flat scan without a vector index.\n- Capture the inline Milvus provider's performance baseline on those workloads. This is\n  time-sensitive: it must be completed before the inline provider removal lands in the\n  OGX distribution.\n- Provision a development pgvector instance (may be manually stood up for the benchmark;\n  no dependency on the production provisioning approach) and run the same workloads\n  against it through the remote vector_io provider path.\n- Compare ingestion and retrieval measurements between the inline Milvus baseline and\n  pgvector; the pass criterion is same order of magnitude, not a specific threshold.\n- Produce a PostgreSQL resource requests/limits recommendation for the default instance,\n  starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource sizing and adjusted\n  per the benchmark results.\n- Document methodology so both measurements are reproducible.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A benchmark report exists documenting the workloads, methodology, the inline Milvus\n  baseline measurements, and the pgvector measurements.\n- The report explicitly states pass/fail against the same-order-of-magnitude criterion\n  for both ingestion and retrieval.\n- The report includes a concrete resource requests/limits recommendation for the default\n  PostgreSQL/pgvector instance, derived from the model-registry-operator reference sizing\n  and the measured results.\n- The inline Milvus baseline is captured before inline providers are removed from the\n  OGX distribution, and the methodology section is sufficient to reproduce both\n  measurements.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is lightweight enough to run\n  in resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead (the\n  sizing recommendation is the deliverable that informs it).\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: no loss of current capability (verified at the\n  order-of-magnitude level against the inline provider baseline).\n- Staff Engineer Input: sprint-1 benchmark scope — capture the inline Milvus baseline\n  and verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude; latency-threshold, corpus-size,\n  and index-type open questions removed; resource sizing references the\n  model-registry-operator.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Time-boxed window: if the inline provider removal lands before the baseline is\n  captured, the comparison loses its reference point and an older distribution image\n  must be used to reconstruct it.\n- If pgvector is not in the same order of magnitude, the default-store approach needs\n  remediation (sizing adjustment, or escalation to reconsider the default) — this is a\n  fix-and-retry outcome recorded in the report, not a re-planning trigger by itself.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":62574,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-899742fad80db074","agent_id":"aecdf30ec811431c0","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"c25408ea-33bb-4c40-b6c2-655fe9cd1640","timestamp":"2026-10-23T20:03:08.703Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
