{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-5f79bdb5-9d94-46a7-84b0-d19869e6eb28","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5c9e951983e2048","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-bgxd3ey4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E004.md","content":"# Document the default vector store and the migration path to production vector databases\n\n## Description\n\nAuthor user-facing documentation for the new default vector store behavior in Gen AI\nStudio: what ships out of the box, its development-experimentation scope, and how to\nmigrate to a production-grade vector database when workloads outgrow the default. This\nepic answers the strategy's open question about a migration path from the default vector\nstore to commonly-used vector database providers.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline (to be authored through the Red Hat docs content process):\n\n- What the default vector store is: a pgvector-backed PostgreSQL instance running as a\n  separate process, scoped per playground namespace, requiring no manual setup or\n  configuration by the user.\n- Scope guidance: the default store is suited to development experimentation with small\n  document sets (flat scan, no vector index); users with production-scale vector\n  workloads should use an external vector database.\n- Migration path from the default store to production vector databases: which external\n  vector database providers are supported through the OGX remote vector_io\n  configuration, the concrete steps and configuration to switch a playground to an\n  external provider, and how stored documents are re-ingested into the new store.\n- Release-notes-facing summary of the change: the inline provider is removed from the\n  OGX distribution and replaced by the platform-level default store, with no additional\n  manual setup steps compared to the previous experience.\n- Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration guidance (user-configured OGXServer\n  connection details), relevant if the selected provisioning approach requires or\n  allows it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Published documentation covers every topic listed in the Scope outline.\n- The migration-path content names the supported external vector database options and\n  gives concrete, verifiable steps and configuration to switch to one, including\n  re-ingestion of previously stored documents.\n- The scope guidance clearly states the development-experimentation positioning and the\n  recommendation to use an external vector database for production-scale workloads.\n- The documentation is technically reviewed against the delivered implementation, with\n  the review sign-off recorded (reviewer and date).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy open question: should users have a migration path from the default vector\n  store to a production-grade vector database, aligned with commonly-used vector DB\n  providers — this epic is the deliverable that answers it.\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: no additional manual setup steps are required for RAG\n  workflows compared to the current experience (documented out-of-the-box behavior).\n- Strategy success criterion: users experience no functional regression when inline\n  providers are removed (documented behavior change and guidance).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The documentation must be written after the provisioning approach is decided and the\n  provider wiring is delivered, so that it describes what was actually built; the\n  technical-review acceptance criterion is the gate that enforces this.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":63505,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-899742fad80db074","agent_id":"aecdf30ec811431c0","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"bfb9976a-85bd-45df-a018-618a61436eed","timestamp":"2026-10-23T20:19:16.643Z","su