{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f87bc917-7282-4923-84fb-45455441c66a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7113d18b9da91d7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-bgxd3ey4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n\n## Description\n\nImplement provisioning of the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly in the\ngen-ai BFF, using the same pattern the BFF already applies when provisioning guardrails\ninfrastructure — for the outcome where the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the\ndashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is not available in the target\nrelease. When a playground with RAG is configured and no database instance exists in the\nnamespace, the BFF creates one; the database runs as a separate process from the OGX\ninstance and is scoped per playground namespace.\n\n## Scope\n\n- BFF logic that creates the PostgreSQL Deployment (pgvector-capable), Service,\n  persistent volume claim, and credentials Secret in the playground's namespace when a\n  playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists, using the existing Red Hat\n  PostgreSQL image that already includes pgvector (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`)\n  — no custom image build.\n- Portability by design: the BFF consumes the database through a connection reference so\n  that, when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available via the DSC in the\n  following release, it can consume the shared connection string instead of\n  self-provisioning, without user-facing change.\n- Lifecycle management design and implementation, giving explicit attention to the facts\n  that the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup of database\n  resources on playground deletion, handling of database pod restarts, and a credential\n  rotation approach.\n- Per-namespace isolation: the database is reachable only within its own namespace.\n- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL\n  reference sizing, adjusted per the sprint-1 benchmark recommendation.\n- Bring-your-own PostgreSQL fallback documentation: user-configured OGXServer connection\n  details, in case BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable during design.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG results in a running PostgreSQL instance with the\n  pgvector extension available in the playground's namespace — including credentials\n  secret and persistent storage — with no user action beyond normal playground setup.\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned database resources.\n- Documented and implemented behavior exists for database pod restarts and credential\n  rotation (the BFF's non-operator lifecycle handling is explicitly designed, not\n  incidental).\n- The BFF consumes the database via a connection reference that can be swapped to a\n  platform-provided shared database without changing user-facing behavior.\n- The database is reachable only from within its own namespace (isolation verified from\n  a second namespace).\n- Resource requests/limits on the provisioned instance match the benchmark-informed\n  recommendation derived from the model-registry-operator reference sizing.\n- No custom container image is built as part of this epic.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: a default vector store is available out-of-the-box\n  without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure.\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store runs as a separate process,\n  decoupled from the OGX instance.\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is scoped appropriately for\n  multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation).\n- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is lightweight enough for\n  resource-constrained environments.\n- Staff Engineer Input: BFF-managed provisioning is the alternative fallback if\n  RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time, mirroring the guardrails provisioning pattern;\n  portability to the 3.6 shared database and lifecycle management (cleanup, restarts,\n  credential rotation) need explicit design attention; bring-your-own PostgreSQL is the\n  further fallback.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This epic exists only on the BFF-managed outcome of the go/no-go decision.\n- Lifecycle management is the design risk the Staff Engineer Input calls out: if the\n  design exceeds a sprint of effort, split lifecycle management (restart/rotation\n  handling) into a follow-up epic rather than expanding this one; if it proves\n  unworkable altogether, fall back to bring-your-own PostgreSQL as documented in the\n  scope.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":66142,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-899742fad80db074","agent_id":"aecdf30ec811431c0","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"effeb1b9-5ad7-4ade-8caf-82ac64d5a782","timestamp":"2026-10-23T21:28:55.124Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
