hmark verifies pgvector is in the\n33\t   same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline; flat scan (no index) suffices.\n34\t6. **Provisioning approach is conditional on RHAISTRAT-7164.** Operator-pattern\n35\t   self-provisioning requires the RHOAI-controlled operator from the dashboard operator\n36\t   modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164, In Progress) — a hard dependency. If it does not land\n37\t   in time, fallbacks are BFF-managed provisioning (guardrails pattern) or bring-your-own\n38\t   PostgreSQL. A go/no-go gate is to be defined in sprint planning.\n39\t7. **Resource sizing** starts from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL\n40\t   requests/limits, adjusted by the sprint-1 benchmark.\n41\t8. **Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC** is the long-term direction but will\n42\t   not be available until the following release; any provisioning built here must be\n43\t   portable to it (consume a connection string rather than assume self-provisioning).\n44\t\n45\t## Epic List\n46\t\n47\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n48\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Go/no-go decision: provisioning approach for the default vector store | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n50\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Configure the pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground OGX instances | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Capture inline Milvus baseline and benchmark pgvector for Playground RAG workloads | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Document the default vector store and the migration path to production vector databases | Implementation (docs-authoring) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Operator-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation (branch A) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005 | End-to-end validation of Playground RAG with the operator-provisioned default vector store | Implementation (branch A) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n55\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation (branch B) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n56\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005 | End-to-end validation of Playground RAG with the BFF-provisioned default vector store | Implementation (branch B) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n57\t\n58\tMain plan: 4 epics (E001-E004). Conditional branches: 4 epics (2 per branch).\n59\t`epic_count` counts main-plan epics only (branch epics are not standalone Jira issues;\n60\tthey are attached to E001 as a plan document).\n61\t\n62\t## Dependency DAG\n63\t\n64\t```mermaid\n65\tgraph TD\n66\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning go/no-go\"]\n67\t    E002[\"E002 Implementation: pgvector provider wiring\"]\n68\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: Milvus baseline + pgvector benchmark\"]\n69\t    E004[\"E004 docs: default store + migration path\"]\n70\t    BRA4[\"BRANCH-A-E004 operator provisioning\"]\n71\t    BRA5[\"BRANCH-A-E005 E2E validation (operator)\"]\n72\t    BRB4[\"BRANCH-B-E004 BFF-managed provisioning\"]\n73\t    BRB5[\"BRANCH-B-E005 E2E validation (BFF)\"]\n74\t\n75\t    E001 --> BRA4\n76\t    E001 --> BRA5\n77\t    E001 --> BRB4\n78\t    E001 --> BRB5\n79\t    E001 --> E004\n80\t    E002 --> BRA5\n81\t    E002 --> BRB5\n82\t    E002 --> E004\n83\t    BRA4 --> BRA5\n84\t    BRB4 --> BRB5\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tE003 (benchmark) is deliberately a root with no outgoing edges: its outcome adjusts\n88\tresource sizing and confirms the order-of-magnitude performance assumption but does not\n89\tchange which epics exist or what they do — per the Step 3 decision rule it is expressed\n90\tas acceptance-criterion gates (\"resource requests/limits per the benchmark\n91\trecommendation\", \"performance within the same order of magnitude\") on the provisioning\n92\tand validation epics, not as DAG edges.\n93\t\n94\tMain-plan critical path length: 2 (E001 → E004, or E002 → E004). Branch chains\n95\t(E001 → BRANCH-x-E004 → BRANCH-x-E005, length 3) are excluded from the main-plan\n96\tcritical path.\n97\t\n98\t## DAG Justification\n99\t\n100\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n101\t|------|------|-----------|\n102\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 3 / Rule 21 | The go/no-go Investigation determines whether the operator-pattern provisioning epic exists at all (RHAISTRAT-7164 uncertain timing). True gate: `remove` if the modularization does not land. Bounded outcomes (≤3) → conditional branch A. |\n103\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E005 | Rule 3 | Validation epic exists in branch A only; gated by the same decision. |\n104\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 / Rule 21 | Same gate, opposite outcome: BFF-managed provisioning exists only if the operator path is not available. True gate: `remove` if the modularization lands. |\n105\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E005 | Rule 3 | Validation epic exists in branch B only. |\n106\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (plus gating) | Docs must describe the selected provisioning approach, so they cannot be authored before the decision resolves. |\n107\t| E002 → BRANCH-A-E005 | Rule 12 | E2E validation exercises the wired pgvector provider configuration; it builds against and verifies that delivered configuration. |\n108\t| E002 → BRANCH-B-E005 | Rule 12 | Same, for the BFF-managed path. |\n109\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic (E002 is the only main-plan Implementation). |\n110\t| BRANCH-A-E004 → BRANCH-A-E005 | Rule 12 | E2E validation runs against the provisioned operator-managed database; the provisioned instance is the artifact validation builds against. |\n111\t| BRANCH-B-E004 → BRANCH-B-E005 | Rule 12 | Same, for the BFF-provisioned database. |\n112\t| (none) E002 ↔ E001 | Rule 14 / config-reference | The provider wiring consumes a connection reference; it is approach-independent (works with operator-provisioned, BFF-provisioned, or user-provided databases) and runs in parallel with the go/no-go decision. |\n113\t| (none) E003 ↔ anything | Step 3 decision rule | Benchmark outcome informs sizing (AC gate), not epic existence or approach. |\n114\t| (none) E001/E002/E003 ↔ konflux-onboarding | — | No Konflux onboarding exists in this decomposition: the Staff Engineer Input eliminated the custom image build (existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image includes pgvector). Rule 25 build-pipeline AC not applicable. |\n115\t\n116\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n117\t\n118\tThe strategy's High Level Requirements section is in the strategy attachment, which was\n119\tnot provisioned in this environment (see Health Warnings). Requirements are therefore\n120\tderived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria and Open Questions, as corrected by the\n121\tStaff Engineer Input. All requirements are P0-equivalent (strategy priority: Blocker);\n122\tno P0/P1/P2 labels were available to split by priority.\n123\t\n124\t| Requirement (source) | Epics | Notes |\n125\t|----------------------|-------|-------|\n126\t| REQ-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/config (AC) | E002, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | Provisioning mechanism is branch-conditional on E001; the wiring (E002) is common. |\n127\t| REQ-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability (AC) | E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 | E003 supplies the order-of-magnitude comparison and baseline; branch validation epics prove no regression. |\n128\t| REQ-3: Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (AC) | E002, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | Remote `remote::pgvector` provider on a separately-deployed PostgreSQL. |\n129\t| REQ-4: Multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation (AC; Open Question resolved by Staff Engineer Input) | BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 | The per-namespace vs per-instance open question is resolved to per-namespace: the Staff Engineer Input prefers per-namespace isolation and removed Auto Rag (the only per-instance consumer) from scope. Residual per-instance needs are an implementation detail, captured as an AC on the provisioning epics. |\n130\t| REQ-5: Lightweight for resource-constrained environments (AC) | E003, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 | Sizing from model-registry-operator reference, adjusted by the E003 benchmark; flat scan, no index. |\n131\t| REQ-6: Migration path from the default store to a production-grade vector database (Open Question, Bram Sundqvist) | E004 | Does not change what is built (the OGX remote vector_io abstraction already standardizes the provider interface) — answered as a documentation deliverable, per the Step 3 decision rule. |\n132\t| ~~AC: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store~~ | — (removed) | **Staff Engineer Input override**: Auto Rag is decoupled; compatibility is addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824's scope. |\n133\t\n134\tFull coverage: every acceptance criterion (post-override) and every open question maps to\n135\tat least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n136\t\n137\t## Health Warnings\n138\t\n139\t- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy body references a full strategy attachment\n140\t  (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in this environment (fetch scripts\n141\t  disabled; only the pre-provisioned strategy file exists). The Affected Components,\n142\t  Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR sections are therefore unavailable. The\n143\t  decomposition is derived from the summary, acceptance criteria, open questions, and the\n144\t  (authoritative) Staff Engineer Input. Impact is limited because the Staff Engineer\n145\t  Input redirects the technical approach in detail, but HLR priority labels\n146\t  (P0/P1/P2) could not be consulted — all requirements are treated as P0-equivalent\n147\t  (strategy priority: Blocker).\n148\t- **Team attribution inferred.** With no Impacted Teams table, the team for all epics is\n149\t  inferred from the strategy label `dashboard-crimson-scrum` (Gen AI Studio scrum team).\n150\t  Verify with the Gen AI Studio lead before scheduling.\n151\t- **Time-sensitive baseline.** E003 must capture the inline Milvus baseline before\n152\t  RHAISTRAT-7474 removes inline providers from the OGX distribution. If the baseline is\n153\t  not captured in time, the order-of-magnitude comparison loses its reference point.\n154\t- **External dependency with uncertain timing.** RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator\n155\t  modularization, In Progress) is a hard dependency for the operator-pattern branch.\n156\t  Per the Staff Engineer Input, this strategy cannot commit to a specific release until\n157\t  that dependency's timing is resolved — this is exactly what E001's go/no-go gate\n158\t  decides. The dependency's timing is outside this strategy's control and belongs to\n159\t  sprint planning, not to any epic's acceptance criteria.\n160\t- **Docs epic cannot DAG-depend on branch epics.** Branch epics are not main-plan nodes,\n161\t  so E004 formally depends only on E001 and E002 (Rule 11 requires every main-plan\n162\t  Implementation). The \"technically reviewed against the delivered implementation\"\n163\t  acceptance criterion on E004 covers the selected branch's outcome.\n164\t- **Component mapping.** All epics are assigned the canonical component `Gen AI Studio`:\n165\t  the implementation work lives in the gen-ai BFF / playground surfaces of\n166\t  odh-dashboard. The OGX distribution itself needs no code change — its remote pgvector\n167\t  vector_io provider already ships in the distribution (corroborated by\n168\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`), so\n169\t  no `OGX Core` epic is generated. The strategy's \"Llama Stack\" naming maps to OGX in\n170\t  the current architecture. Auto Rag was removed from components per Staff Engineer\n171\t  Input (would otherwise have been `AutoRAG`).\n172\t- **Epic size / re-split trigger.** BRANCH-B-E004 bundles provisioning with lifecycle\n173\t  management (cleanup, pod-restart handling, credential rotation). The Staff Engineer\n174\t  Input flags lifecycle as needing explicit design attention. Re-split trigger: if the\n175\t  BFF lifecycle design exceeds roughly a sprint of effort, split lifecycle management\n176\t  (restart/rotation handling) into a follow-up epic rather than expanding the\n177\t  provisioning epic further.\n178\t- **Priority note (not an inversion).** E004 (docs) is P0 via the docs-authoring\n179\t  priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority `Blocker` mapped to P0, since\n180\t  Blocker is the highest severity, above Critical). It is exempt from priority\n181\t  inheritance and does not push priority upstream.\n182\t\n183\t## Tiered Delivery\n184\t\n185\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-7164) has uncertain timing, so the strategy is split:\n186\t\n187\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E002 (pgvector provider wiring —\n188\t  approach-independent, the vector_io integration the Staff Engineer Input says is\n189\t  largely reusable) and E003 (inline Milvus baseline capture + pgvector benchmark +\n190\t  sizing recommendation — risk-reduction validation with a time-boxed window). E001\n191\t  also starts immediately to bound the uncertainty as early as sprint planning allows.\n192\t- **Tier 2 (gated on the go/no-go decision):** branch provisioning (BRANCH-A-E004 or\n193\t  BRANCH-B-E004) and the corresponding E2E validation (BRANCH-x-E005), plus the\n194\t  documentation (E004) once the approach is known and the wiring delivered.\n195\t\n196\t## Conditional Decomposition\n197\t\n198\tE001 has two bounded outcomes that change which downstream epics exist:\n199\t\n200\t- **Branch A — operator path** (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time): BRANCH-A-E004\n201\t  (operator-managed provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern) and\n202\t  BRANCH-A-E005 (E2E validation). `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`: if the\n203\t  modularization does not land in time, this branch is dropped in favor of Branch B.\n204\t- **Branch B — BFF-managed path** (RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time):\n205\t  BRANCH-B-E004 (BFF-managed provisioning following the guardrails-infrastructure\n206\t  pattern) and BRANCH-B-E005 (E2E validation). `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`: if\n207\t  the modularization lands in time, this branch is dropped in favor of Branch A. If\n208\t  BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable during design, the recorded\n209\t  fallback is bring-your-own PostgreSQL (user-configured OGXServer connection details,\n210\t  consistent with the OGX 1.0 breaking-changes posture) — noted as the branch's\n211\t  fallback approach rather than a third branch, since it requires no new provisioning\n212\t  implementation (configuration documentation only).\n213\t\n214\tBoth branches include their own E2E validation epic because validation depends on the\n215\tspecific branch's provisioning work; a main-plan epic must not depend on a branch epic.\n216\tBranch epics are not standalone Jira issues — they attach to E001 as a plan document.\n217\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4737d4ae1466a3","agent_id":"af7df5a568cec00a3","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"0b52190d-6ae1-4ade-934d-730db2837c62","timestamp":"2026-10-24T03:10:33.732Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd30552f01d4255a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
