{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aca1f6456536d8d5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: 'Go/no-go decision: provisioning approach for the default vector store'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Go/no-go decision: provisioning approach for the default vector store\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation resolves how the default pgvector-backed vector store is provisioned\n30\tfor Gen AI Studio playgrounds in the target release. Three approaches are possible:\n31\tself-provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (available only if the dashboard\n32\toperator modularization strategy, RHAISTRAT-7164, lands in time), self-provisioning\n33\tmanaged by the gen-ai BFF (the pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails\n34\tinfrastructure), or requiring users to bring their own PostgreSQL. The investigation\n35\tdefines the go/no-go gate criteria in sprint planning, assesses the dependency's delivery\n36\toutlook, and produces a decision record that selects the implementation path and\n37\tunblocks downstream provisioning and validation work.\n38\t\n39\t## Scope\n40\t\n41\t- Define the go/no-go gate criteria for the dashboard operator modularization dependency\n42\t  (RHAISTRAT-7164): the delivery milestones that must hold for the operator-pattern\n43\t  approach to be viable for the target release, and the date at which the gate is\n44\t  decided as part of sprint planning.\n45\t- Assess the delivery status and forecast of RHAISTRAT-7164 with the owning team,\n46\t  gathering evidence for the gate decision.\n47\t- Evaluate the two self-provisioning options against:\n48\t  - lifecycle-management requirements: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod\n49\t    restarts, credential rotation (the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop,\n50\t    so these need explicit design attention on the BFF path);\n51\t  - portability to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL planned via the DSC for the\n52\t    following release (each option must be able to consume a connection string rather\n53\t    than assume self-provisioning forever);\n54\t  - per-namespace isolation requirements for multi-tenant clusters.\n55\t- Decide the provisioning approach and record the decision, including the fallback to\n56\t  bring-your-own PostgreSQL (user-configured OGXServer connection details) if neither\n57\t  self-provisioning option is viable for the release.\n58\t- Communicate the outcome so the selected implementation path is scheduled and the\n59\t  unselected alternatives are dropped from the plan.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- A written decision record exists that states the go/no-go gate criteria, the evidence\n64\t  used (delivery plan and status of the dashboard operator modularization dependency),\n65\t  and the selected provisioning approach.\n66\t- The decision names exactly one selected approach (operator-pattern self-provisioning,\n67\t  BFF-managed self-provisioning, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL) and identifies the gate\n68\t  owner and the gate decision date.\n69\t- The decision record documents the lifecycle-management and portability considerations\n70\t  that informed the choice, and the fallback approach if the selected option fails\n71\t  during implementation.\n72\t- The decision is socialized with the Gen AI Studio team and the owning team of the\n73\t  operator modularization dependency, and the release plan reflects the selected path.\n74\t\n75\t## HLR Traceability\n76\t\n77\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: a default vector store is available out-of-the-box\n78\t  without manual provisioning — this investigation selects the mechanism that delivers it.\n79\t- Strategy dependency noted in Staff Engineer Input: RHAISTRAT-7164 is a hard dependency\n80\t  for the operator path; the strategy cannot commit to a release until its timing is\n81\t  resolved.\n82\t\n83\t## Risks / Notes\n84\t\n85\t- The gating input — the delivery timing of RHAISTRAT-7164 — is controlled by another\n86\t  team and cannot be compressed by this epic. The gate decision date is a sprint-planning\n87\t  commitment, not an engineering deliverable; delays there delay the entire Tier 2 chain.\n88\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4737d4ae1466a3","agent_id":"af7df5a568cec00a3","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"26a970a1-8b64-4673-8d15-f79c77c0dcb2","timestamp":"2026-10-24T03:10:37.941Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aca1f6456536d8d5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
