{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f6e3173c58994d5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E004\n3\ttitle: Document the default vector store and the migration path to production vector\n4\t  databases\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tpriority: P0\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n14\tai_signals:\n15\t  change_specificity: 1\n16\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n17\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n18\t  existing_foundation: 1\n19\t  open_questions: 0\n20\t  external_dependency: 0\n21\t  human_process_gates: -1\n22\t  repo_access: 1\n23\t  architecture_claims: 1\n24\tinvestigation_signals: null\n25\tai_implementability: High\n26\tai_implementability_score: 4\n27\tjira_key: null\n28\tbranch: null\n29\tgated_by: null\n30\tgate_failure_impact: null\n31\t---\n32\t# Document the default vector store and the migration path to production vector databases\n33\t\n34\t## Description\n35\t\n36\tAuthor user-facing documentation for the new default vector store behavior in Gen AI\n37\tStudio: what ships out of the box, its development-experimentation scope, and how to\n38\tmigrate to a production-grade vector database when workloads outgrow the default. This\n39\tepic answers the strategy's open question about a migration path from the default vector\n40\tstore to commonly-used vector database providers.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\tContent outline (to be authored through the Red Hat docs content process):\n45\t\n46\t- What the default vector store is: a pgvector-backed PostgreSQL instance running as a\n47\t  separate process, scoped per playground namespace, requiring no manual setup or\n48\t  configuration by the user.\n49\t- Scope guidance: the default store is suited to development experimentation with small\n50\t  document sets (flat scan, no vector index); users with production-scale vector\n51\t  workloads should use an external vector database.\n52\t- Migration path from the default store to production vector databases: which external\n53\t  vector database providers are supported through the OGX remote vector_io\n54\t  configuration, the concrete steps and configuration to switch a playground to an\n55\t  external provider, and how stored documents are re-ingested into the new store.\n56\t- Release-notes-facing summary of the change: the inline provider is removed from the\n57\t  OGX distribution and replaced by the platform-level default store, with no additional\n58\t  manual setup steps compared to the previous experience.\n59\t- Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration guidance (user-configured OGXServer\n60\t  connection details), relevant if the selected provisioning approach requires or\n61\t  allows it.\n62\t\n63\t## Acceptance Criteria\n64\t\n65\t- Published documentation covers every topic listed in the Scope outline.\n66\t- The migration-path content names the supported external vector database options and\n67\t  gives concrete, verifiable steps and configuration to switch to one, including\n68\t  re-ingestion of previously stored documents.\n69\t- The scope guidance clearly states the development-experimentation positioning and the\n70\t  recommendation to use an external vector database for production-scale workloads.\n71\t- The documentation is technically reviewed against the delivered implementation, with\n72\t  the review sign-off recorded (reviewer and date).\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability\n75\t\n76\t- Strategy open question: should users have a migration path from the default vector\n77\t  store to a production-grade vector database, aligned with commonly-used vector DB\n78\t  providers — this epic is the deliverable that answers it.\n79\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: no additional manual setup steps are required for RAG\n80\t  workflows compared to the current experience (documented out-of-the-box behavior).\n81\t- Strategy success criterion: users experience no functional regression when inline\n82\t  providers are removed (documented behavior change and guidance).\n83\t\n84\t## Risks / Notes\n85\t\n86\t- The documentation must be written after the provisioning approach is decided and the\n87\t  provider wiring is delivered, so that it describes what was actually built; the\n88\t  technical-review acceptance criterion is the gate that enforces this.\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4737d4ae1466a3","agent_id":"af7df5a568cec00a3","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"1d30d579-5b47-45cf-ab52-3cf44c85aee9","timestamp":"2026-10-24T03:10:43.188Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f6e3173c58994d5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
