circular edges; no\nmain-plan epic depends on a branch epic; both branch files set `branch` and\n`gated_by`; no konflux-onboarding epics exist (Staff Engineer Input eliminated\nthe image build), so Rules 7/8/10 are N/A; E002's consumption of the provisioning\nepics' connection secret is correctly an approach-independent AC, not an edge\n(Rule 13 config-reference). Critical path (main-plan 2, branch chain 3) is\nreasonable for an M strategy.\n\nHowever, there is a critical missing-edge issue:\n\n- **E003's deliverables are consumed by downstream epics with no DAG edges.**\n  BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 have acceptance criteria requiring resource\n  requests/limits to \"match the benchmark-informed recommendation\" — E003's\n  sizing deliverable. BRANCH-A-E005 and BRANCH-B-E005 have acceptance criteria\n  requiring performance \"within the same order of magnitude as the captured\n  inline Milvus baseline\" — E003's baseline deliverable — and both epics' Risks\n  sections state outright: \"Requires the pgvector provider wiring, the\n  [provisioning], and the inline Milvus baseline to be in place before the\n  comparison scenarios can run.\" That is a data/artifact dependency stated in\n  epic content and absent from both the DAG and the consumers' `dependencies`.\n  The decomposition's defense — the Step 3 decision rule expressing the outcome\n  as AC gates — is the carve-out that applies to build-pipeline outputs consumed\n  as content (image digests, attestations, endpoint URLs); an investigation's\n  benchmark report consumed as a verification baseline is not in that category.\n  Correction: add `E003 → BRANCH-A-E004`, `E003 → BRANCH-B-E004`,\n  `E003 → BRANCH-A-E005`, `E003 → BRANCH-B-E005` edges — or, if E003 is\n  reclassified per the Criterion 4 finding, record the AC-gate rationale under\n  the rule that actually covers a verification-time reference; as written the\n  shape satisfies neither rule.\n\n- Minor: if E003 is reclassified as an Implementation (see Criterion 4), Rule 11\n  additionally requires E004 (docs-authoring) to depend on it, since the docs'\n  scope-guidance and positioning content draws on the benchmark conclusions.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nAll epics are component `Gen AI Studio`, team `dashboard-crimson-scrum` — correct\ngiven the strategy label and the Staff Engineer Input's redirection of all work\nto the gen-ai BFF / operator layer (the OGX distribution itself needs no code\nchange, corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/.../ogx-distribution.md`).\nSplits follow work-product and sub-system axes: provider wiring (E002) vs\nprovisioning (branch E004s) vs validation (branch E005s) vs docs (E004). The\nBRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 overlap is not duplication — they are mutually\nexclusive branch alternatives, never coexisting. No code/content mixing within\nany epic. BRANCH-B-E004's provisioning-plus-lifecycle bundling exceeds the\n~2-week guideline risk, but the decomposition records a concrete re-split\ntrigger in Health Warnings (\"if the BFF lifecycle design exceeds roughly a\nsprint of effort, split lifecycle management into a follow-up epic\") — a managed\nwatch item, not a defect. No epic is plausibly multi-sprint beyond that.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n\n- **Critical — E003 is misclassified as Investigation.** The review test: does\n  the outcome change which downstream epics exist or what they do? The\n  decomposition's own DAG justification answers no — \"its outcome adjusts\n  resource sizing and confirms the order-of-magnitude performance assumption but\n  does not change which epics exist or what they do\" — and E003's Risks section\n  calls a failed comparison \"a fix-and-retry outcome... not a re-planning\n  trigger.\" No epic lists E003 in `dependencies` and nothing is gated by it: an\n  Investigation with zero dependents. Per decompose Rule 4, an informational\n  unknown that doesn't change what gets built is \"not a true Investigation\" —\n  reclassify as an acceptance criterion or as an Implementation producing a\n  deliverable. E003 produces concrete deliverables (benchmark report, baseline\n  measurements, sizing recommendation), so the Implementation-that-produces-a-\n  deliverable shape fits. Correction: retype E003 as Implementation (with\n  `ai_signals` in place of `investigation_signals`) and resolve its DAG\n  treatment per the Criterion 2 finding.\n- **Major — E004 lists Investigation E001 in `dependencies` with\n  `gated_by: null`.** The summary's own edge justification describes the edge as\n  \"Rule 11 (plus gating)\" — \"docs must describe the selected provisioning\n  approach, so they cannot be authored before the decision resolves\" — i.e., the\n  decision rewrites E004's scope. An Investigation dependency without\n  `gated_by` is a metadata gap; automated consumers detect gates from\n  `gated_by`. Correction: set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001` with\n  `gate_failure_impact.action: rewrite` (content changes with the selected\n  approach) and a fallback approach.\n\nE001 itself is a genuine Investigation (the go/no-go decision determines which\nbranch epics exist), all branch epics correctly carry `gated_by: E001` with both\n`action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and every `gated_by` target appears\nin the epic's direct `dependencies`.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal fields are type-appropriate throughout (`investigation_signals` on E001\nand E003; `ai_signals` on E002, E004, and all four branch epics), and rationale\nfiles exist for every epic. Spot-checks hold up: E001's\n`human_judgment_required: -2` matches the Staff Engineer Input explicitly making\nthe go/no-go a sprint-planning decision; E001's `source_accessibility: 0` is\nright — the gating fact is a delivery forecast, not a readable artifact;\nBRANCH-A-E004's `external_dependency: -1` (RHAISTRAT-7164) and\n`existing_foundation: 0` (operator layer not yet delivered) are justified;\nBRANCH-B-E004's `open_questions: -1` (unresolved lifecycle design) is justified;\nE004's `human_process_gates: -1` (docs process + review sign-off) is justified.\n`architecture_claims` calibration verified against `.context`: the\nogx-distribution.md file confirms vector I/O through Milvus/PGVector/Qdrant\nwith environment-variable provider guards (E002, E004, branch E005 rationales);\nmodel-registry-operator.md confirms controller-created `{registry-name}-postgres`\nDeployments with credentials Secrets (BRANCH-A-E004); odh-dashboard.md confirms\nthe gen-ai BFF's Kubernetes API interaction (BRANCH-B-E004). Note: if E003 is\nretyped per Criterion 4, its signal set must be converted to the 9-signal\nImplementation rubric — but as scored against its declared type, the values are\nconsistent.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nRule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E002 (a replacement of the\ninline-provider capability) carries the reversibility/rollback AC; E004\n(docs-authoring) carries the technical-review-with-sign-off AC; Rule 25 is N/A\n(no konflux chain, correctly noted in the DAG justification). E003's\n\"baseline captured before inline providers are removed\" AC is a condition on\nthe epic's own deliverable (the capture is its work), with the timing risk\nproperly recorded in Risks/Notes — not a misfiled external-precondition AC. No\ndraft-ID references (E001 etc.) appear in any epic body. All ACs are testable\nand derived from strategy ACs plus Staff Engineer Input.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nEvery post-override acceptance criterion, success criterion, and open question\nmaps to epic work: out-of-the-box availability (E002 + branch E004s), no\ncapability loss (E003 + branch E005s), separate-process decoupling (E002 +\nbranch E004s), multi-tenant scoping (branch E004s/E005s, with the per-namespace\nresolution of the open question recorded in the traceability matrix),\nlightweight sizing (E003 + branch E004s), migration path (E004), and RHAISTRAT-7474\nunblocking (the plan as a whole). The Auto Rag removal is a documented Staff\nEngineer override, not a silent drop. Conditional branches cover all three\nbounded outcomes of E001 — operator path (Branch A), BFF path (Branch B), and\nbring-your-own PostgreSQL handled as Branch B's documented fallback plus E004's\nBYO configuration guidance, which is defensible since BYO requires no new\nprovisioning implementation. Staff Engineer corrections (SQLite storage,\nexisting pgvector image, no Konflux, storage independence, order-of-magnitude\nbenchmark) are all carried into epic scope. Health Warnings properly record the\nmissing attachment, inferred team, time-sensitive baseline, and the 7164\nexternal dependency.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":53650,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4737d4ae1466a3","agent_id":"af7df5a568cec00a3","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"5bef277b-5ea0-46fb-99e4-7df14ae944d0","timestamp":"2026-10-24T23:34:34.286Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
