 gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 with gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite and a fallback_approach\n32\t    so automated consumers can detect the gate.'\n33\terror: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8004\n36\t\n37\t## Review Summary\n38\t\n39\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n40\tRecommendation: revise\n41\t\n42\tContract validator: OK (epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11\n43\tdocs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all\n44\tpass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with 4 main-plan\n45\tepics — correct. The failures below are the two structural issues the validator\n46\tcannot check: the E003 DAG treatment and the E003 epic type.\n47\t\n48\t## Criterion Details\n49\t\n50\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n51\t\n52\tThe strategy's HLR section is in the missing attachment; the decomposition derived\n53\tREQ-1 through REQ-6 from the strategy's acceptance criteria and open questions as\n54\tcorrected by the Staff Engineer Input, and documented this in Health Warnings. All\n55\tsix requirements map to at least one epic, and the removed Auto Rag AC is recorded\n56\tas a Staff Engineer override with its destination (RHAISTRAT-7824). Every epic's\n57\tHLR Traceability section ties back to strategy ACs. All requirements are\n58\tP0-equivalent (strategy priority: Blocker), so no priority split or bundling\n59\tanalysis was required. Priority inheritance holds: E001 gates all Tier 2 work and\n60\tis P0. E004 (docs-authoring) is P0 via the docs priority exception (Blocker mapped\n61\tabove Critical to P0 — reasonable reading of the mapping). No orphaned\n62\trequirements.\n63\t\n64\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n65\t\n66\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: every edge in the mermaid diagram\n67\t(`graph TD`, dependency → dependent) has a matching `dependencies` entry in the\n68\ttarget epic's frontmatter and vice versa; no invalid or circular edges; no\n69\tmain-plan epic depends on a branch epic; both branch files set `branch` and\n70\t`gated_by`; no konflux-onboarding epics exist (Staff Engineer Input eliminated\n71\tthe image build), so Rules 7/8/10 are N/A; E002's consumption of the provisioning\n72\tepics' connection secret is correctly an approach-independent AC, not an edge\n73\t(Rule 13 config-reference). Critical path (main-plan 2, branch chain 3) is\n74\treasonable for an M strategy.\n75\t\n76\tHowever, there is a critical missing-edge issue:\n77\t\n78\t- **E003's deliverables are consumed by downstream epics with no DAG edges.**\n79\t  BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 have acceptance criteria requiring resource\n80\t  requests/limits to \"match the benchmark-informed recommendation\" — E003's\n81\t  sizing deliverable. BRANCH-A-E005 and BRANCH-B-E005 have acceptance criteria\n82\t  requiring performance \"within the same order of magnitude as the captured\n83\t  inline Milvus baseline\" — E003's baseline deliverable — and both epics' Risks\n84\t  sections state outright: \"Requires the pgvector provider wiring, the\n85\t  [provisioning], and the inline Milvus baseline to be in place before the\n86\t  comparison scenarios can run.\" That is a data/artifact dependency stated in\n87\t  epic content and absent from both the DAG and the consumers' `dependencies`.\n88\t  The decomposition's defense — the Step 3 decision rule expressing the outcome\n89\t  as AC gates — is the carve-out that applies to build-pipeline outputs consumed\n90\t  as content (image digests, attestations, endpoint URLs); an investigation's\n91\t  benchmark report consumed as a verification baseline is not in that category.\n92\t  Correction: add `E003 → BRANCH-A-E004`, `E003 → BRANCH-B-E004`,\n93\t  `E003 → BRANCH-A-E005`, `E003 → BRANCH-B-E005` edges — or, if E003 is\n94\t  reclassified per the Criterion 4 finding, record the AC-gate rationale under\n95\t  the rule that actually covers a verification-time reference; as written the\n96\t  shape satisfies neither rule.\n97\t\n98\t- Minor: if E003 is reclassified as an Implementation (see Criterion 4), Rule 11\n99\t  additionally requires E004 (docs-authoring) to depend on it, since the docs'\n100\t  scope-guidance and positioning content draws on the benchmark conclusions.\n101\t\n102\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n103\t\n104\tAll epics are component `Gen AI Studio`, team `dashboard-crimson-scrum` — correct\n105\tgiven the strategy label and the Staff Engineer Input's redirection of all work\n106\tto the gen-ai BFF / operator layer (the OGX distribution itself needs no code\n107\tchange, corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/.../ogx-distribution.md`).\n108\tSplits follow work-product and sub-system axes: provider wiring (E002) vs\n109\tprovisioning (branch E004s) vs validation (branch E005s) vs docs (E004). The\n110\tBRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 overlap is not duplication — they are mutually\n111\texclusive branch alternatives, never coexisting. No code/content mixing within\n112\tany epic. BRANCH-B-E004's provisioning-plus-lifecycle bundling exceeds the\n113\t~2-week guideline risk, but the decomposition records a concrete re-split\n114\ttrigger in Health Warnings (\"if the BFF lifecycle design exceeds roughly a\n115\tsprint of effort, split lifecycle management into a follow-up epic\") — a managed\n116\twatch item, not a defect. No epic is plausibly multi-sprint beyond that.\n117\t\n118\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n119\t\n120\t- **Critical — E003 is misclassified as Investigation.** The review test: does\n121\t  the outcome change which downstream epics exist or what they do? The\n122\t  decomposition's own DAG justification answers no — \"its outcome adjusts\n123\t  resource sizing and confirms the order-of-magnitude performance assumption but\n124\t  does not change which epics exist or what they do\" — and E003's Risks section\n125\t  calls a failed comparison \"a fix-and-retry outcome... not a re-planning\n126\t  trigger.\" No epic lists E003 in `dependencies` and nothing is gated by it: an\n127\t  Investigation with zero dependents. Per decompose Rule 4, an informational\n128\t  unknown that doesn't change what gets built is \"not a true Investigation\" —\n129\t  reclassify as an acceptance criterion or as an Implementation producing a\n130\t  deliverable. E003 produces concrete deliverables (benchmark report, baseline\n131\t  measurements, sizing recommendation), so the Implementation-that-produces-a-\n132\t  deliverable shape fits. Correction: retype E003 as Implementation (with\n133\t  `ai_signals` in place of `investigation_signals`) and resolve its DAG\n134\t  treatment per the Criterion 2 finding.\n135\t- **Major — E004 lists Investigation E001 in `dependencies` with\n136\t  `gated_by: null`.** The summary's own edge justification describes the edge as\n137\t  \"Rule 11 (plus gating)\" — \"docs must describe the selected provisioning\n138\t  approach, so they cannot be authored before the decision resolves\" — i.e., the\n139\t  decision rewrites E004's scope. An Investigation dependency without\n140\t  `gated_by` is a metadata gap; automated consumers detect gates from\n141\t  `gated_by`. Correction: set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001` with\n142\t  `gate_failure_impact.action: rewrite` (content changes with the selected\n143\t  approach) and a fallback approach.\n144\t\n145\tE001 itself is a genuine Investigation (the go/no-go decision determines which\n146\tbranch epics exist), all branch epics correctly carry `gated_by: E001` with both\n147\t`action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and every `gated_by` target appears\n148\tin the epic's direct `dependencies`.\n149\t\n150\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n151\t\n152\tSignal fields are type-appropriate throughout (`investigation_signals` on E001\n153\tand E003; `ai_signals` on E002, E004, and all four branch epics), and rationale\n154\tfiles exist for every epic. Spot-checks hold up: E001's\n155\t`human_judgment_required: -2` matches the Staff Engineer Input explicitly making\n156\tthe go/no-go a sprint-planning decision; E001's `source_accessibility: 0` is\n157\tright — the gating fact is a delivery forecast, not a readable artifact;\n158\tBRANCH-A-E004's `external_dependency: -1` (RHAISTRAT-7164) and\n159\t`existing_foundation: 0` (operator layer not yet delivered) are justified;\n160\tBRANCH-B-E004's `open_questions: -1` (unresolved lifecycle design) is justified;\n161\tE004's `human_process_gates: -1` (docs process + review sign-off) is justified.\n162\t`architecture_claims` calibration verified against `.context`: the\n163\togx-distribution.md file confirms vector I/O through Milvus/PGVector/Qdrant\n164\twith environment-variable provider guards (E002, E004, branch E005 rationales);\n165\tmodel-registry-operator.md confirms controller-created `{registry-name}-postgres`\n166\tDeployments with credentials Secrets (BRANCH-A-E004); odh-dashboard.md confirms\n167\tthe gen-ai BFF's Kubernetes API interaction (BRANCH-B-E004). Note: if E003 is\n168\tretyped per Criterion 4, its signal set must be converted to the 9-signal\n169\tImplementation rubric — but as scored against its declared type, the values are\n170\tconsistent.\n171\t\n172\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n173\t\n174\tRule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E002 (a replacement of the\n175\tinline-provider capability) carries the reversibility/rollback AC; E004\n176\t(docs-authoring) carries the technical-review-with-sign-off AC; Rule 25 is N/A\n177\t(no konflux chain, correctly noted in the DAG justification). E003's\n178\t\"baseline captured before inline providers are removed\" AC is a condition on\n179\tthe epic's own deliverable (the capture is its work), with the timing risk\n180\tproperly recorded in Risks/Notes — not a misfiled external-precondition AC. No\n181\tdraft-ID references (E001 etc.) appear in any epic body. All ACs are testable\n182\tand derived from strategy ACs plus Staff Engineer Input.\n183\t\n184\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n185\t