{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a00dc4fdfca0611e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005\n3\ttitle: End-to-end validation of Playground RAG with the operator-provisioned default\n4\t  vector store\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: A\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n15\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004\n16\tgate_failure_impact:\n17\t  action: remove\n18\t  fallback_approach: If the operator path is not selected, this validation epic is\n19\t    replaced by the equivalent validation against whichever provisioning approach\n20\t    the go/no-go decision selects.\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: 1\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n25\t  existing_foundation: 1\n26\t  open_questions: 1\n27\t  external_dependency: 0\n28\t  human_process_gates: 0\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: High\n34\tai_implementability_score: 6\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\t---\n37\t# End-to-end validation of Playground RAG with the operator-provisioned default vector store\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tValidate the complete out-of-the-box experience on the operator path: Gen AI Studio\n42\tplayground RAG workflows function against the operator-managed default pgvector-backed\n43\tvector store with no loss of current capability and no manual setup steps, in\n44\tmulti-tenant and resource-constrained conditions. This epic exists only on the\n45\toperator-path outcome of the go/no-go decision.\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- End-to-end test scenarios for playground RAG against the default store: document\n50\t  upload, embedding, indexing, retrieval, and grounded generation.\n51\t- Regression comparison against the current capability set: every playground RAG\n52\t  capability available with the inline provider passes with the default store.\n53\t- Multi-tenant verification: two playgrounds in different namespaces cannot access each\n54\t  other's vector data (per-namespace isolation).\n55\t- Resource-constrained environment verification: the playground and its default store\n56\t  operate within the benchmark-informed resource envelope on a constrained cluster.\n57\t- Disconnected-install validation of the full default-store path (image reference\n58\t  override plus playground RAG workflow).\n59\t- Performance sanity check against the captured inline Milvus baseline: ingestion and\n60\t  retrieval within the same order of magnitude (development-experimentation scope; no\n61\t  latency SLAs).\n62\t- A published validation report summarizing results and any remediation taken.\n63\t\n64\t## Acceptance Criteria\n65\t\n66\t- A freshly configured playground with RAG works end to end with zero manual database\n67\t  setup or configuration steps.\n68\t- The full existing playground RAG capability set passes the end-to-end suite against\n69\t  the default store with no functional regression.\n70\t- Per-namespace isolation is verified: cross-namespace access to another playground's\n71\t  vector data fails.\n72\t- The playground and its default store operate within the recommended resource envelope\n73\t  on a resource-constrained cluster.\n74\t- The disconnected-install scenario passes with the PostgreSQL image reference pointed\n75\t  at an internal registry mirror.\n76\t- Measured ingestion and retrieval performance is within the same order of magnitude as\n77\t  the captured inline Milvus baseline.\n78\t- The validation report is published and states pass/fail for each criterion above.\n79\t\n80\t## HLR Traceability\n81\t\n82\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with\n83\t  the default vector store with no loss of current capability.\n84\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: a default vector store is available out-of-the-box\n85\t  without manual provisioning or configuration.\n86\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is scoped appropriately for\n87\t  multi-tenant environments.\n88\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is lightweight enough for\n89\t  resource-constrained environments.\n90\t- Strategy success criterion: users experience no functional regression when inline\n91\t  providers are removed from the OGX distribution.\n92\t- Staff Engineer Input: validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG\n93\t  workflows (Auto Rag compatibility is out of scope, deferred to RHAISTRAT-7824);\n94\t  benchmark criterion is order-of-magnitude, not a threshold.\n95\t\n96\t## Risks / Notes\n97\t\n98\t- Requires the pgvector provider wiring, the operator-managed provisioning, and the\n99\t  inline Milvus baseline to be in place before the comparison scenarios can run.\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97085c80c2e4a002","agent_id":"aa401dc28139df828","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"62a30fa5-6896-471e-9186-45b41c5d19c9","timestamp":"2026-10-25T00:07:25.993Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a00dc4fdfca0611e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b64afd72ef2dfe5f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: remove\n15\t  fallback_approach: If RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time, drop this branch in favor of\n16\t    operator-pattern provisioning; if BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable\n17\t    during design, fall back to bring-your-own PostgreSQL (user-configured OGXServer\n18\t    connection details, documentation only).\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tImplement provisioning of the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly in the\n40\tgen-ai BFF, using the same pattern the BFF already applies when provisioning guardrails\n41\tinfrastructure — for the outcome where the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the\n42\tdashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is not available in the target\n43\trelease. When a playground with RAG is configured and no database instance exists in the\n44\tnamespace, the BFF creates one; the database runs as a separate process from the OGX\n45\tinstance and is scoped per playground namespace.\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- BFF logic that creates the PostgreSQL Deployment (pgvector-capable), Service,\n50\t  persistent volume claim, and credentials Secret in the playground's namespace when a\n51\t  playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists, using the existing Red Hat\n52\t  PostgreSQL image that already includes pgvector (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`)\n53\t  — no custom image build.\n54\t- Portability by design: the BFF consumes the database through a connection reference so\n55\t  that, when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available via the DSC in the\n56\t  following release, it can consume the shared connection string instead of\n57\t  self-provisioning, without user-facing change.\n58\t- Lifecycle management design and implementation, giving explicit attention to the facts\n59\t  that the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup of database\n60\t  resources on playground deletion, handling of database pod restarts, and a credential\n61\t  rotation approach.\n62\t- Per-namespace isolation: the database is reachable only within its own namespace.\n63\t- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL\n64\t  reference sizing, adjusted per the sprint-1 benchmark recommendation.\n65\t- Bring-your-own PostgreSQL fallback documentation: user-configured OGXServer connection\n66\t  details, in case BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable during design.\n67\t\n68\t## Acceptance Criteria\n69\t\n70\t- Configuring a playground with RAG results in a running PostgreSQL instance with the\n71\t  pgvector extension available in the playground's namespace — including credentials\n72\t  secret and persistent storage — with no user action beyond normal playground setup.\n73\t- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned database resources.\n74\t- Documented and implemented behavior exists for database pod restarts and credential\n75\t  rotation (the BFF's non-operator lifecycle handling is explicitly designed, not\n76\t  incidental).\n77\t- The BFF consumes the database via a connection reference that can be swapped to a\n78\t  platform-provided shared database without changing user-facing behavior.\n79\t- The database is reachable only from within its own namespace (isolation verified from\n80\t  a second namespace).\n81\t- Resource requests/limits on the provisioned instance match the benchmark-informed\n82\t  recommendation derived from the model-registry-operator reference sizing.\n83\t- No custom container image is built as part of this epic.\n84\t\n85\t## HLR Traceability\n86\t\n87\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: a default vector store is available out-of-the-box\n88\t  without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure.\n89\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store runs as a separate process,\n90\t  decoupled from the OGX instance.\n91\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is scoped appropriately for\n92\t  multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation).\n93\t- Strategy acceptance criterion: the default vector store is lightweight enough for\n94\t  resource-constrained environments.\n95\t- Staff Engineer Input: BFF-managed provisioning is the alternative fallback if\n96\t  RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time, mirroring the guardrails provisioning pattern;\n97\t  portability to the 3.6 shared database and lifecycle management (cleanup, restarts,\n98\t  credential rotation) need explicit design attention; bring-your-own PostgreSQL is the\n99\t  further fallback.\n100\t\n101\t## Risks / Notes\n102\t\n103\t- This epic exists only on the BFF-managed outcome of the go/no-go decision.\n104\t- Lifecycle management is the design risk the Staff Engineer Input calls out: if the\n105\t  design exceeds a sprint of effort, split lifecycle management (restart/rotation\n106\t  handling) into a follow-up epic rather than expanding this one; if it proves\n107\t  unworkable altogether, fall back to bring-your-own PostgreSQL as documented in the\n108\t  scope.\n109\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97085c80c2e4a002","agent_id":"aa401dc28139df828","session_id":"dea9077a-5606-465d-8634-40ab1d74f5b7","uuid":"23eae637-ceda-40d0-be52-4e04ceaaa11f","timestamp":"2026-10-25T00:07:26.001Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b64afd72ef2dfe5f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
